<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00395</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ec0e56e0-83a8-4cd2-84e3-37bcc99e531d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="1f209805-d7b2-47b1-8d9f-e26f07909497"><problem><para>
      <para>Which of the following points is the solution to the system of equations that is graphed?
          <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f8b2817150ab876b42893d7d192fa7208d9b2dfc"/></media></figure></para>
      </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((20,0)\)</para><para>\((0,20)\)</para><para>\(45,0)\)</para><para>\((5,15)\)</para></problem><solution><para>\((5,15)\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is \((5, 15)\).
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For questions 5 – 8, use the following scenario and matching table.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>A streaming service has two different membership plans. Option A charges $10 per month plus $5 for each movie. Option B charges $20 a month then $2 a movie.</para>
</blockquote>
<table class="os-raise-doubleheadertable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        <para>Number of movies in a month, \(x\).</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        <para>0</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        <para>1</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        <para>2</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        <para>3</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        <para>4</para>
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        <para>Option A</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry>
        <para>10</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>15</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>20</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>25</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>30</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        <para>Option B</para>
      </emphasis></entry><entry>
        <para>20</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>22</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>24</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>26</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>28</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="a77aa2bc-c28c-4a82-bce2-f92d629bcdf3"><problem><para>
      <para> What is the \(y\)-intercept for Option A and Option B? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here.</para>
    </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(Option\;\ A: 10\\ Option\;\ B: 20\)</para><para>\(Option\;\ A: 20\\ Option\;\ B: 10\)</para><para>\(Option\;\ A: 5\\ Option\;\ B: 2\)</para><para>\(Option\;\ A: 2\\ Option\;\ B: 5\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(Option\;\ A: 10\\ Option\;\ B: 20\)</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is the \(y\)-intercepts are Option A = 10 and Option B = 20.
      </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For questions 9 - 12, use the following situation and graph.</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>A family buys a total of 32 tickets at a carnival. Ride tickets cost $1.50 each and food tickets cost $3.25 each. The family pays a total of $90 for the tickets. The situation is graphed below.</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/eb44767d0c6710228091a100debe7800c1ed2a24"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>The \(x\)-axis represents the number of ride tickets and the \(y\)-axis represents the number of food tickets.</para>
</blockquote>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="e7633643-d341-4b95-9151-a83dc6c45559"><problem><para>
      <para>Which line depicts the equation that represents the statement “A family buys a total of 32 tickets at a carnival”?</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>The orange, solid line</para><para>
      The correct answer is the orange, solid line, which represents \(x + y = 32\).
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
</document>
